
{"id":"89096f24-e45d-4965-bec0-ec4b75cf024c","object":"chat.completion","created":1785030157,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    lengths = case['lengths']\\n    scores = case['scores']\\n    budget = case['budget']\\n    n = len(lengths)\\n    INF_NEG = -1e18\\n    # forward DP: dp[i][t] = max score using first i items (0..i-1) with exactly t tokens\\n    dp = [[INF_NEG] * (budget + 1) for _ in range(n + 1)]\\n    dp[0][0] = 0.0\\n    for i in range(n):\\n        length = lengths[i]\\n        score = scores[i]\\n        for t in range(budget + 1):\\n            if dp[i][t] > INF_NEG / 2:\\n                # exclude\\n                if dp[i+1][t] < dp[i][t]:\\n                    dp[i+1][t] = dp[i][t]\\n                # include\\n                newt = t + length\\n                if newt <= budget:\\n                    newscore = dp[i][t] + score\\n                    if dp[i+1][newt] < newscore:\\n                        dp[i+1][newt] = newscore\\n    best_score = max(dp[n])\\n    # find minimal tokens used among those achieving best_score\\n    optimal_tokens = min(t for t in range(budget + 1) if abs(dp[n][t] - best_score) < 1e-9)\\n    \\n    # backward DP: dp_rev[i][t] = max score using items i..n-1 with exactly t tokens\\n    dp_rev = [[INF_NEG] * (budget + 1) for _ in range(n + 1)]\\n    dp_rev[n][0] = 0.0\\n    for i in range(n - 1, -1, -1):\\n        length = lengths[i]\\n        score = scores[i]\\n        for t in range(budget + 1):\\n            if dp_rev[i+1][t] > INF_NEG / 2:\\n                # exclude current item\\n                if dp_rev[i][t] < dp_rev[i+1][t]:\\n                    dp_rev[i][t] = dp_rev[i+1][t]\\n                # include current item\\n                newt = t + length\\n                if newt <= budget:\\n                    newscore = dp_rev[i+1][t] + score\\n                    if dp_rev[i][newt] < newscore:\\n                        dp_rev[i][newt] = newscore\\n    \\n    # reconstruct lexicographically smallest list\\n    selected_indices = []\\n    tokens_sofar = 0\\n    score_sofar = 0.0\\n    for i in range(n):\\n        include_feasible = False\\n        exclude_feasible = False\\n        # check include\\n        if tokens_sofar + lengths[i] <= budget:\\n            rem_tokens = optimal_tokens - tokens_sofar - lengths[i]\\n            rem_score = best_score - score_sofar - scores[i]\\n            if rem_tokens >= 0 and rem_score >= -1e-9:\\n                if abs(dp_rev[i+1][rem_tokens] - rem_score) < 1e-9:\\n                    include_feasible = True\\n        # check exclude\\n        rem_tokens_excl = optimal_tokens - tokens_sofar\\n        rem_score_excl = best_score - score_sofar\\n        if rem_tokens_excl >= 0 and rem_score_excl >= -1e-9:\\n            if abs(dp_rev[i+1][rem_tokens_excl] - rem_score_excl) < 1e-9:\\n                exclude_feasible = True\\n        if include_feasible and exclude_feasible:\\n            # choose include for lexicographically smaller list\\n            selected_indices.append(i)\\n            tokens_sofar += lengths[i]\\n            score_sofar += scores[i]\\n        elif include_feasible:\\n            selected_indices.append(i)\\n            tokens_sofar += lengths[i]\\n            score_sofar += scores[i]\\n        # else exclude (do nothing)\\n    return {\\n        \\\"selected_indices\\\": selected_indices,\\n        \\\"tokens_used\\\": tokens_sofar,\\n        \\\"total_score\\\": score_sofar\\n    }\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement `solve(case)` for task NLP-CSE-02. The problem: given arrays `lengths` (token lengths of sentences), `scores` (salience scores), and a `budget` (max tokens), select a subset of indices (preserving original order) to maximize total score without exceeding budget. Break ties by fewer tokens used, then lexicographic source indices (i.e., the selected indices in ascending order? or the order of selection? Probably lexicographic order of the list of selected indices, meaning if two selections have same score and same tokens used, the one that comes first in lexicographic order when comparing the sorted list of indices wins. Since indices are sorted because we keep order, that means the selected list itself is sorted ascending. So lexicographic comparison of the list of indices: compare first element, then second, etc. So we need to pick the solution that is lexicographically smallest list of indices among those with max score and min tokens.\n\nWe need to use dynamic programming. Since it's a knapsack with items (sentences) in order, we can do DP over positions and budgets. But we also need to reconstruct the chosen subset with tie-breaking. We'll compute DP[i][t] = max total score using first i items (0-indexed, i from 0 to n) and exactly t tokens? Or up to t? Budget is exact budget? The task says \"under an exact token budget\" and \"Never exceed the budget\". It's typical to allow less than budget? \"under an exact token budget\" might mean the total tokens used must be <= budget, but \"exact budget\" could mean we must use exactly the budget? The description: \"Select and reconstruct context-scored sentences under an exact token budget\". However, the examples: For case 0: lengths sum to 3+4+1+4+2+3=17, budget=11, so cannot use all. Likely we can use <= budget. In tie-breaking: \"fewer used tokens\" suggests we prefer solutions that use fewer tokens among those with same score. So we allow under-using budget. So we can use any total tokens <= budget.\n\nWe need DP that tracks maximum score for each (item, tokens_used) and also stores information to reconstruct the chosen indices, and also to break ties.\n\nApproach: DP over items. For each item i (0-indexed), and each possible tokens_used t from 0 to budget, we store best score achievable using first i+1 items (considering up to item i, inclusive). Also we need to store the chosen indices for that state to break ties. Since there can be many solutions, we need to propagate tie-breaking information.\n\nAlternatively, we can compute DP that for each i and t, stores (score, tokens_used, list_of_indices) and when updating, if scores equal compare by tokens_used (less is better) then by lexicographic order of indices. But storing full list for each DP cell is memory heavy (n*budget). n can be up to? Not given, but probably manageable. In Python, we can store tuples of (score, tokens_used, list) but that's heavy.\n\nBetter: Use DP that stores only score and a \"path\" or \"predecessor\" info. Then at the end, we reconstruct the best solution by backtracking. Since we have tie-breaking rules, we need to define which predecessor to choose when scores and tokens_used are equal. The tie-breaking rules: maximize score; if equal score, minimize tokens_used; if equal, lexicographically smallest indices. Lexicographic order of the list of selected indices means we want the earliest possible indices in the sorted order. Since we process items in order, if we have two solutions with same score and same tokens used, we want the one that is lexicographically smaller. That is equivalent to at each step, when we have a choice to include or exclude an item, we need to compare the resulting lists. But that's complex.\n\nWe can treat the problem as: find the best subset according to lexicographic order of the list of selected indices (sorted), after primary and secondary criteria. One way: perform DP that for each state keeps the best achievable list of indices (as tuple) when only considering items up to current. Since we are breaking ties by lexicographic order of the full list, and we process items in order, we can decide inclusion/exclusion based on the full future effect. However, a simpler method: since the tie-breaking is lexicographic on the list of indices (sorted), and we are selecting indices in order, the lexicographic comparison is equivalent to comparing the binary selection vector (1 for selected, 0 for not) from left to right: the first index where they differ, the one with 1 is larger? Actually lexicographic on the list of indices: [i1, i2, ...] sorted. Compare first element: smaller index is better? Let's think: suppose two solutions A and B both have same score and same tokens used. Their selected index lists are sorted ascending. Compare first element: if A's first index < B's first index, then A is lexicographically smaller. That means A includes an earlier sentence than B. That seems plausible. So we want to include earlier sentences if possible. That is equivalent to preferring to include items as early as possible when scores and tokens are equal.\n\nIn DP, we can incorporate tie-breaking by when scores and tokens are equal, we compare the paths. But DP from start to end: when we consider adding an item i, the new list is old list + [i]. The lexicographic order of two lists can be compared by comparing the first differing element. However, if we are comparing two partial solutions that end at different items, it's not straightforward.\n\nAnother approach: Since n is likely not huge, we can store for each DP cell (i,t) the best score and also a bitmask or encoded path. But number of items might be up to maybe 20? Not sure. The fixtures show up to 7 items. Could be larger. But we need a robust solution.\n\nMaybe we can use DP that computes the maximum score for each (i,t) and also the minimum tokens used for that score, and then later reconstruct the lexicographically smallest list. Since after we know the max score and the tokens used (min among max score), we can do a greedy reconstruction: go through items from left to right, and decide whether to include it if it's part of some optimal solution with the remaining budget and future items, and with lexicographic preference. This is typical for lexicographically smallest solution in knapsack: after DP, we can trace back, and at each step when both include and exclude give same score and tokens, we choose include (because earlier index is smaller) as long as it is feasible. So we can compute DP for all i,t: best score, and also maybe a second DP to know if including an item leads to optimal solution.\n\nSimpler: compute DP[i][t] = max score using first i items (0..i-1) with exactly t tokens. We can also compute a separate DP for max score with any tokens up to budget. But we need to also track min tokens for max score. We can compute DP as dictionary or list of lists. For each i from 0 to n, for each t from 0 to budget, store (score, tokens_used, indices_tuple). But memory.\n\nAlternatively, we can just store score and tokens_used, and then reconstruct by backtracking with a rule: at each step, we want to know whether including item i-1 (0-indexed) leads to an optimal solution for the remaining capacity. We can compute DP table of scores and tokens_used, and also compute the maximum future score that can be achieved from a given state (like from item i onward with remaining budget). Then during reconstruction, we go forward, and at each item, we check if including it is part of some optimal solution that also respects tie-breaking: i.e., if including yields the same max score as excluding, and same tokens_used (or fewer tokens?), we need to prefer include because it gives a lexicographically smaller list (since earlier index). But careful: lexicographic order compares the full list; including an earlier index makes the first element smaller (or if the first element is the same, then compare second, etc). So if we have a choice between include and exclude that lead to same score and same total tokens, we should include because that yields a list that starts with a smaller index (since the included index is the current i, while exclude would have a later index as first). However, the number of tokens used may differ. The tie-breaking says: first maximize score, then minimize tokens used, then lexicographic indices. So if two solutions have same score but different tokens, the one with fewer tokens is better regardless of indices. So during reconstruction, we need to be aware of token count. But we already know the optimal tokens_used (minimum among max score). So we can compute the optimal tokens_used from DP. Then reconstruction: we want to find the lexicographically smallest list of indices among those achieving optimal score and optimal tokens_used. That reconstruction can be done greedily: for each item i from 0 to n-1, decide whether to include it. We need to know if there exists an optimal solution (with score = optimal_score, tokens = optimal_tokens) that includes item i, given the decisions already made and the remaining capacity. We can precompute a reverse DP: max score from item i onward with given budget. Then we can iterate forward and decide include if there exists a solution that includes i and achieves overall optimum, and if there is also a solution that excludes i and achieves optimum, we need to compare which yields lexicographically smaller list. The lexicographic comparison is: the one that includes earlier items is smaller. So if both include and exclude can achieve the optimum, we should include because it gives a smaller first index (i) compared to the first index in the exclude path (which would be larger than i, since we skipped i and the next selected index will be > i). So we should include if feasible. However, we must also consider token count: if including uses tokens that would force later to use more tokens? But we already fixed optimal tokens_used total. So we need to ensure that there is a completion from i+1 onward that uses exactly (optimal_tokens_used - length[i]) tokens and achieves (optimal_score - score[i]). If that is possible, and also there is a completion from i+1 onward that uses exactly optimal_tokens_used tokens and achieves optimal_score (excluding item i), then both are feasible. But we may need to know if the include path yields the lexicographically smaller list. As argued, include is smaller because it includes i, which is less than any index in the exclude path. So we choose include. If include is not feasible, we must exclude. If exclude is not feasible, we must include. This greedy approach works if we have deterministic DP that can tell us whether a certain state can lead to optimum. We can compute DP forward and also backward (from end to start) to get best score and min tokens for given remaining budget. Then during forward reconstruction, we decide based on the existence of a path.\n\nBut we also need to handle the case where scores and tokens are equal but the include and exclude paths might have the same indices? That's not possible because include has i and exclude doesn't. So include always yields a lexicographically smaller list because its first selected index is i, while the exclude path's first selected index is at least i+1. So in tie-breaking, include is strictly better, so always choose include if feasible with optimal score and tokens.\n\nThus reconstruction algorithm:\n1. Compute DP_forward: for i from 0 to n, for t from 0 to budget, store max_score and the min_tokens? Actually we need to know for each state (i used, remaining budget), what is the best score achievable from that state to the end. Better to compute DP backward: dp_back[i][t] = (max_score, min_tokens_used) for items from i to end, starting with t tokens remaining. Then during forward reconstruction, we simulate.\n\nBut we also need to consider that tokens_used is total across all selected. We need to compute globally optimal score and min tokens among max score. Let's compute forward DP: dp[i][c] = max score using first i items (0..i-1) with exactly c tokens used. Initialize dp[0][0]=0, others -inf. For each item i (0-indexed), for each c, dp[i+1][c] = max(dp[i][c], dp[i][c-lengths[i]] + scores[i] if c>=lengths[i]). Also track for each state, the tokens used? We can also compute the best score for each i and c. After loop, overall best score = max_{c<=budget} dp[n][c]. Let best_score = that. Then find min_tokens = min{c where dp[n][c] == best_score}. That's the optimal tokens used.\n\nNow we need to reconstruct lexicographically smallest list achieving best_score and min_tokens. We can do forward selection with knowledge of dp_back. Compute dp_back: similar but reversed. dp_back[i][c] = max score achievable from item i to end (i..n-1) with exactly c tokens used? Or with remaining budget c. Let's define dp_back[i][r] = max score using items i..n-1 with total tokens exactly r (or ≤ r). We want exactly r? We'll use exactly r for matching total tokens. We'll compute dp_back for each i (0-indexed start) and r from 0 to budget. We can compute from end to start.\n\nBut we can also reconstruct using only forward dp and a greedy check: at step i (0-indexed), we have already selected some items and used tokens_sofar, and we have a current list indices_sofar. We need to decide whether to include item i. We know best_score and optimal_tokens. We need to check if there exists a solution from i+1 to end that uses exactly (optimal_tokens - tokens_sofar - lengths[i]) tokens and achieves score (best_score - score_sofar - scores[i]) if we include i. Similarly for exclude: check if there exists solution from i+1 with (optimal_tokens - tokens_sofar) tokens and score (best_score - score_sofar). If both are possible, choose include because lexicographically smaller. If only one is possible, choose that.\n\nTo check existence for a subproblem (from index i+1, with remaining tokens rem, and required remaining score rem_score), we need to know if there is a subset from i+1..n-1 that uses exactly rem tokens and achieves exactly rem_score. Since scores can be floating? In the fixture, scores are floats, but they are given as decimals. They might be real numbers. However, we should compare with tolerance? Typically, the DP will handle floats as equal if exact, but we need to be careful with floating point equality. The scores are given as 0.73228615 etc. They might be exact (float64). In DP, we can compare using exact equality because we are not performing arithmetic that introduces rounding errors? Adding floats can cause small errors. To be safe, we can represent scores as integers scaled by some factor? But we don't know precision. Alternatively, we can use tuples (score, tokens) and compare with tolerance? But the problem says return summed score as number. If we compute DP exactly with floats, it should be fine for the given fixtures. In practice, we can treat scores as floats and compare with a small epsilon (1e-9) for equality when reconstructing. But for simplicity, we can assume that the scores are exact and the DP's additions will produce exact results as long as we don't have rounding issues. However, floating point addition may cause tiny errors. For safety, we can use tuple of (score, -tokens) as the comparison key, and when checking equality, we can compare after rounding to some decimals. Since we need to return the exact sum, we can compute it from the selected indices.\n\nBetter: We can avoid float equality issues by storing the total score as a float and comparing with a tolerance. But during reconstruction, we need to check if a certain score is achievable. We can use DP that stores the best score as a float, and when checking existence we can see if the dp value is within epsilon of the target. However, that might be problematic. Alternative: Since the scores come from the input, we can treat them as exact values and compute total score by summing original floats. If we keep the sums exact (adding the same floats), the total will be exactly the sum of the inputs. So no rounding error if we accumulate using the same values. So if we compute DP by summing the actual values, and then later we use the same values to compute total score, it should match exactly. So we can use exact equality.\n\nSo we will implement DP that returns float scores, and we'll compare equality with exact ==. For safety, we can still use math.isclose with a very small relative tolerance. But for simplicity, we'll use exact.\n\nNow, DP for backward: We need to compute dp_rev[i][r] = max score from items i to n-1 using exactly r tokens. But we also need to know the minimal tokens used for that max score? Actually for checking existence, we only need to know if there is a subset that achieves exactly a required score with exactly required tokens. Since we already know optimal total score and optimal total tokens, the required remaining score and tokens are exact. So we need to know if dp_rev[i+1][required_tokens] == required_score. However, there could be multiple combinations with same score but different tokens? But we require exact tokens. So we just check equality. However, note that the dp_rev may store the max score for each exact token count. But we need to check that there is some subset with exactly those tokens that achieves that score. If the dp_rev value for that token count equals required_score, then it's feasible. But there could be multiple subsets achieving that score with that token count; we don't need to distinguish further because our forward greedy will always prefer include when both are feasible (and that will lead to lexicographically smallest because include gives smaller index). However, there is a nuance: when both include and exclude are feasible, we choose include. But what if include and exclude both lead to the same final list? That's impossible. So it's fine.\n\nBut we must ensure that dp_rev correctly computes the max score for each exact token count. We can compute it as:\nInitialize dp_rev[n][0] = 0, others -inf.\nFor i from n-1 down to 0:\n  for r from 0 to budget:\n    dp_rev[i][r] = max(dp_rev[i+1][r], dp_rev[i+1][r - lengths[i]] + scores[i] if r>=lengths[i])\nWe'll use -inf for impossible states.\n\nNow, reconstruction:\ntokens_sofar = 0\nscore_sofar = 0\nselected = []\nfor i in range(n):\n    # consider including item i\n    include_feasible = False\n    if tokens_sofar + lengths[i] <= budget:\n        required_tokens_total = optimal_tokens - tokens_sofar - lengths[i]\n        required_score_total = optimal_score - score_sofar - scores[i]\n        if required_tokens_total >= 0 and required_score_total >= 0:\n            # check if dp_rev[i+1][required_tokens_total] == required_score_total (with tolerance)\n            if abs(dp_rev[i+1][required_tokens_total] - required_score_total) < 1e-9:\n                include_feasible = True\n\n    # exclude feasible\n    required_tokens_total_excl = optimal_tokens - tokens_sofar\n    required_score_total_excl = optimal_score - score_sofar\n    exclude_feasible = False\n    if required_tokens_total_excl >= 0 and required_score_total_excl >= 0:\n        if abs(dp_rev[i+1][required_tokens_total_excl] - required_score_total_excl) < 1e-9:\n            exclude_feasible = True\n\n    if include_feasible and exclude_feasible:\n        # Choose include because lexicographically smaller index\n        selected.append(i)\n        tokens_sofar += lengths[i]\n        score_sofar += scores[i]\n    elif include_feasible:\n        selected.append(i)\n        tokens_sofar += lengths[i]\n        score_sofar += scores[i]\n    else:\n        # exclude\n        pass\n\nAfter loop, we have selected. Verify that sum scores and tokens match optimal. Return { \"selected_indices\": selected, \"tokens_used\": tokens_sofar, \"total_score\": score_sofar }\n\nEdge cases: budget might be 0, all lengths positive? lengths given as positive integers. Scores positive? Not necessarily, but assume positive. If no items fit within budget? Then best_score=0, optimal_tokens=0, selected empty. Our algorithm should handle that: initial dp_rev will have 0 at r=0. Reconstruction: tokens_sofar=0, score_sofar=0. For each i, include_feasible and exclude_feasible will be? optimal_tokens=0, so include_feasible requires tokens_sofar+lengths[i] <= budget? budget might be >0 but lengths[i] might be >0, so tokens_sofar+lengths[i] would exceed optimal_tokens? Actually optimal_tokens is 0, so for include we need required_tokens_total = 0 - 0 - lengths[i] = -lengths[i] <0, not feasible. exclude_feasible: required_tokens_total = 0-0=0, required_score_total=0, check dp_rev[i+1][0] should be 0 (empty set from i+1). So exclude_feasible true. So we'll exclude all items. Good.\n\nNow, we need to consider floating point equality. We used abs diff <1e-9. That should be fine given that scores are given with 8 decimal places, sums should be exact up to 1e-8 or so. We can also use math.isclose with rel_tol=1e-9, abs_tol=1e-12.\n\nImplementation details:\n\n- Function solve(case): case is a dict with keys \"lengths\", \"scores\", \"budget\". Also \"_case_nonce\" but ignore.\n- n = len(lengths)\n- budget = case[\"budget\"]\n- Use DP arrays as list of lists of floats, initialized to -inf (use -1e18 or float('-inf')).\n- DP forward for score only (to find global optimal):\n    dp = array of size (n+1) x (budget+1) filled with -inf\n    dp[0][0] = 0\n    for i in range(n):\n        for t in range(budget+1):\n            if dp[i][t] > -1e9:\n                # exclude\n                if dp[i+1][t] < dp[i][t]:\n                    dp[i+1][t] = dp[i][t]\n                # include\n                newt = t + lengths[i]\n                if newt <= budget:\n                    newscore = dp[i][t] + scores[i]\n                    if dp[i+1][newt] < newscore:\n                        dp[i+1][newt] = newscore\n    best_score = max(dp[n][:budget+1])\n    # find min tokens among those achieving best_score\n    optimal_tokens = min(t for t in range(budget+1) if abs(dp[n][t] - best_score) < 1e-9)\n\n- DP backward (dp_rev) similar:\n    dp_rev = array of size (n+1) x (budget+1) filled with -inf\n    dp_rev[n][0] = 0\n    for i in range(n-1, -1, -1):\n        for t in range(budget+1):\n            if dp_rev[i+1][t] > -1e9:\n                # exclude current item\n                if dp_rev[i][t] < dp_rev[i+1][t]:\n                    dp_rev[i][t] = dp_rev[i+1][t]\n                # include current item\n                newt = t + lengths[i]\n                if newt <= budget:\n                    newscore = dp_rev[i+1][t] + scores[i]\n                    if dp_rev[i][newt] < newscore:\n                        dp_rev[i][newt] = newscore\n    # Note: dp_rev[i][t] now holds max score using items i..n-1 with exactly t tokens.\n\n- Reconstruction as above.\n\nWe must ensure that we use float('-inf') correctly. Use -1e18 or use None and check.\n\nNow, write the Python code as a string for the JSON envelope.\n\nAlso note: The solution should be a JSON object with key \"implementation\". So final output: {\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    ...\"}. We need to escape newlines, backslashes, quotes for JSON. We'll generate the Python code as a string.\n\nPotential performance: n and budget are small (from fixtures, budget up to 12, lengths small). So O(n*budget) is fine.\n\nWe'll write the code with proper indentation.\n\nLet's write the solution.\n\nWe'll also need to import math for isclose? Not necessary, we can use abs diff.\n\nEdge: if all scores are zero? Then best_score=0, optimal_tokens=0, selected empty.\n\nNow produce final JSON."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":1141,"completion_tokens":6478,"total_tokens":7619,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":1024},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":5498},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":1024,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":117},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}